  "prediction": "**Rare Nirvana Photos and Web Preservation Efforts Make Headlines**\n\nIn recent news, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed and shared by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem who hosted the fledging outfit's first show in his basement. The photos, which feature Kurt Cobain and Poukkula's father playing together, were discovered in Poukkula's father's biographies of Cobain and Nirvana. The photos come on the heels of a deluge of rare Cobain and Nirvana artifacts, many of which were incorporated into Brett Morgen's acclaimed documentary, Montage of Heck.\n\nMeanwhile, the Internet Archive is working to preserve web pages and fix broken links on the web. The organization is using crawls to archive pages as they are created and archive the pages that they refer to, allowing users to access the original content even if the referenced pages are changed or taken down. This effort aims to preserve the integrity of online content and provide a permanent record of the web's history.\n\nIn related news, Montage of Heck, a documentary about Kurt Cobain's life, is set to return to movie theaters starting August 7th. The film, which features unprecedented access to Cobain's archives, has been well-received by critics and audiences alike.\n\nOverall, these stories highlight the importance of preserving cultural heritage and online content, as well as the enduring legacy of Nirvana and Kurt Cobain's music.",
